# The program hands `kept` to nobody, so linked() rules out of the literal's layout that its objects gain a property, and compiled code lists its keys without a look.
# `poke` is a free name that each run defines, and it is handed nothing. It takes the object out of the heap (aotFindObjectOfLayout()), a road that no program has, and stores an ELEMENT on it:
# under an index, by the engine's own push, by Object.assign from a list. Each comes to Object::putByIndex() and goes by the short way of elements, which holds EVERY layout to what is proven of it,
# as the long road does: the run time ends the process at the deed. Once the short way asked that of layouts of classes alone, and all three went on in silence.
# OPTIONS: mechanisms=all
# RUN(STORES): run-in-shell globalThis.poke = () => { aotFindObjectOfLayout(0)[0] = 1; print("went on"); };
# STORES: 1 one count,label
# STORES: AOT: AddProperty of an object of layout L0
# STORES-NOT: went on
# STORES: crashed with
# RUN(PUSHES): run-in-shell globalThis.poke = () => { Array.prototype.push.call(aotFindObjectOfLayout(0), 1); print("went on"); };
# PUSHES: AOT: AddProperty of an object of layout L0
# PUSHES-NOT: went on
# PUSHES: crashed with
# RUN(ASSIGNS): run-in-shell globalThis.poke = () => { Object.assign(aotFindObjectOfLayout(0), [1]); print("went on"); };
